INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving mill running an air-jet loom notices that switching from a standard relay nozzle timing profile to a ''staggered'' relay nozzle firing sequence (nozzles firing in a precisely sequenced wave rather than simultaneously) improves weft insertion reliability at very high loom widths. Why does staggered (sequenced) relay nozzle firing outperform simultaneous firing for wide looms specifically?', '["Nozzle firing sequence has no relationship to weft insertion reliability at any loom width, making this observed improvement coincidental","A staggered firing sequence creates a more continuous, wave-like air propulsion ''push'' that follows the weft''s leading tip as it travels across the shed, maintaining more consistent propulsion force along the ENTIRE width — simultaneous firing of all relay nozzles at once creates uneven propulsion (strong near each nozzle, weaker between them), which becomes an increasingly serious problem as loom width increases and the weft must travel a longer total distance relying on this relay assistance","Staggered firing always REDUCES total air consumption to zero, with the weft insertion actually achieved through an entirely different, unrelated mechanical mechanism","Simultaneous nozzle firing is always superior at any loom width, and this scenario''s described improvement from staggered firing is factually implausible"]'::jsonb, 'A staggered firing sequence creates a more continuous, wave-like air propulsion ''push'' that follows the weft''s leading tip as it travels across the shed, maintaining more consistent propulsion force along the ENTIRE width — simultaneous firing of all relay nozzles at once creates uneven propulsion (strong near each nozzle, weaker between them), which becomes an increasingly serious problem as loom width increases and the weft must travel a longer total distance relying on this relay assistance', 'This extends the earlier air-jet weaving discussion with the specific engineering solution to air-jet''s width limitation — relay nozzles (positioned along the loom width to re-boost the weft''s air propulsion as it travels) work best when fired in a SEQUENCED, staggered pattern that times each nozzle''s burst to arrive just as the weft''s leading tip reaches that position, creating a continuous ''traveling wave'' of propulsion rather than uneven pulses — this precisely-timed sequencing is exactly what allows air-jet technology to be extended to wider looms than would otherwise be reliable with simpler simultaneous firing.', 3, '{"relay-nozzle-timing"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A rapier weaving machine uses a flexible rapier band (rather than a rigid rapier) for weft insertion. What SPECIFIC mechanical advantage does a flexible band offer over a rigid rapier, particularly relevant to WIDE looms?', '["Flexible bands offer no mechanical advantage over rigid rapiers at any loom width, making the choice between them purely arbitrary","A flexible band can be COILED/stored on a take-up reel as it retracts (rather than needing to physically withdraw as a straight rigid rod the full insertion distance), dramatically reducing the physical space/mechanism needed on EACH side of a wide loom, since a rigid rapier for a very wide loom would require an impractically long straight retraction path on both sides","Rigid rapiers are always faster than flexible bands at any given loom width, making rigid rapiers the objectively superior choice with no situational tradeoff","Flexible bands can only be used for narrow looms under 1 meter wide, making them technically inapplicable to the wide-loom scenario the question describes"]'::jsonb, 'A flexible band can be COILED/stored on a take-up reel as it retracts (rather than needing to physically withdraw as a straight rigid rod the full insertion distance), dramatically reducing the physical space/mechanism needed on EACH side of a wide loom, since a rigid rapier for a very wide loom would require an impractically long straight retraction path on both sides', 'This is a genuine mechanical-engineering insight into rapier system design — a rigid rapier rod must physically retract in a straight line for the FULL insertion distance, meaning a very wide loom would require an impractically long straight mechanism space on each side just for rapier storage/retraction; a flexible band, by contrast, can curve and coil onto a compact take-up reel as it retracts, dramatically reducing the physical footprint needed — this is precisely why flexible rapier band systems became the practical standard for wider looms, while rigid rapier systems remain more common on narrower width machines where this space constraint is less significant.', 3, '{"flexible-vs-rigid-rapier"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving technologist explains that increasing WARP TENSION (within a reasonable range) on a loom can sometimes IMPROVE weaving efficiency (fewer stoppages) even though higher tension might intuitively seem more likely to cause MORE warp breaks. Under what condition does increasing tension actually REDUCE breakage rather than increase it?', '["Increasing tension always increases breakage with zero possible exception, making this scenario technically impossible under any condition","If the ORIGINAL tension was actually TOO LOW (causing poor, inconsistent shed formation, excessive warp end movement/entanglement, or poor filling insertion due to a poorly-defined shed opening), a moderate tension INCREASE can improve shed clarity and warp end control, actually REDUCING breakage caused by these low-tension-related problems — beyond the OPTIMAL tension point, further increases would then start increasing breakage from pure over-tension stress, meaning there''s a genuine optimum, not a simple ''always worse'' relationship","Warp tension has no relationship to shed formation clarity or warp end control at any tension level, making this proposed explanation technically unfounded","Only INFINITE tension can ever reduce breakage, making any finite, practical tension adjustment irrelevant to this discussion"]'::jsonb, 'If the ORIGINAL tension was actually TOO LOW (causing poor, inconsistent shed formation, excessive warp end movement/entanglement, or poor filling insertion due to a poorly-defined shed opening), a moderate tension INCREASE can improve shed clarity and warp end control, actually REDUCING breakage caused by these low-tension-related problems — beyond the OPTIMAL tension point, further increases would then start increasing breakage from pure over-tension stress, meaning there''s a genuine optimum, not a simple ''always worse'' relationship', 'This is a genuine, non-obvious insight about warp tension''s relationship to breakage — like the earlier optimum-twist-multiplier concept, warp tension has an OPTIMUM point, not a simple monotonic ''more tension = more breaks'' relationship: too-LOW tension causes its own distinct problems (poor shed clarity, end entanglement, poor filling insertion from an ill-defined shed), and a moderate INCREASE from an originally too-low starting point can genuinely IMPROVE weaving performance by fixing these low-tension problems — only PAST the optimum point does further tension increase start causing pure over-tension-driven breakage, which is why correct tension setting requires finding this optimum empirically rather than assuming ''lower is always safer.''', 3, '{"warp-tension-optimum"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A loom''s ''pick finding'' or ''pick repair'' mechanism (used after a stoppage, to correctly re-align the loom''s timing back to the exact point of the last correctly-inserted pick before resuming) is critical for avoiding which SPECIFIC defect that would otherwise occur on EVERY single stoppage-restart?', '["Pick-finding mechanisms have no real defect-prevention purpose and exist purely to speed up the mechanical restart process, unrelated to fabric quality","Without accurate pick-finding, restarting the loom without exact re-synchronization risks either a MISSING pick (skipped entirely) or a DOUBLE pick (the same weft position picked twice) at the restart point, both of which are visible, function-affecting fabric defects — beyond the already-discussed ''starting mark'' tension/appearance issue, incorrect pick alignment would compound this into an actual structural defect at every single stoppage, which would be far more frequent and severe without accurate pick-finding","Pick-finding mechanisms are only relevant for hand-operated looms, with zero application to modern automated shuttleless weaving machines","A missing or double pick has no visible or functional effect on finished fabric, making pick-finding mechanisms an unnecessary engineering feature with no real defect-prevention value"]'::jsonb, 'Without accurate pick-finding, restarting the loom without exact re-synchronization risks either a MISSING pick (skipped entirely) or a DOUBLE pick (the same weft position picked twice) at the restart point, both of which are visible, function-affecting fabric defects — beyond the already-discussed ''starting mark'' tension/appearance issue, incorrect pick alignment would compound this into an actual structural defect at every single stoppage, which would be far more frequent and severe without accurate pick-finding', 'This extends the earlier starting-mark discussion to a MORE severe potential defect — while starting marks arise from tension/beating-force differences at restart (a cosmetic/appearance issue), an actual MISALIGNED restart (without precise pick-finding) risks a genuine STRUCTURAL defect: either skipping a pick entirely (missing pick) or repeating the same weft position (double pick) — since stoppages happen routinely on any real production loom (even a well-run loom has SOME stop frequency), accurate pick-finding mechanisms are essential precisely because without them, EVERY stoppage would risk introducing one of these more serious structural defects, not just the milder cosmetic starting-mark issue.', 3, '{"pick-finding-mechanism"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A technologist compares ''positive'' weft feeder devices (used on modern shuttleless looms to pre-measure and store a precise length of weft yarn before each insertion, released under controlled tension) against simply pulling weft directly from a stationary supply package during insertion. What SPECIFIC problem does a positive weft feeder solve that direct-pull supply cannot?', '["Positive weft feeders solve no real problem and are purely a cost-adding feature with zero functional benefit over direct-pull supply","Direct-pull weft insertion draws yarn under unwinding tension that varies with the supply package''s remaining diameter/fill level and unwind dynamics (similar to the earlier let-off tension-drift concern) — a positive feeder pre-measures and stores each pick''s exact length under CONTROLLED, consistent tension BEFORE insertion begins, decoupling the actual insertion tension from the variable, less predictable dynamics of directly unwinding from a supply package, giving much more consistent pick-to-pick tension and therefore more consistent PPI/fabric quality","Positive weft feeders are only used for producing zero-weft fabric, an application with no actual weft insertion required at all","Weft tension consistency has no relationship to fabric PPI or quality, making the entire premise of needing a positive feeder technically unfounded"]'::jsonb, 'Direct-pull weft insertion draws yarn under unwinding tension that varies with the supply package''s remaining diameter/fill level and unwind dynamics (similar to the earlier let-off tension-drift concern) — a positive feeder pre-measures and stores each pick''s exact length under CONTROLLED, consistent tension BEFORE insertion begins, decoupling the actual insertion tension from the variable, less predictable dynamics of directly unwinding from a supply package, giving much more consistent pick-to-pick tension and therefore more consistent PPI/fabric quality', 'This directly parallels the earlier let-off tension-drift discussion (where friction-brake let-off tension varies with beam diameter) but applied to weft supply — pulling weft directly from a stationary package means insertion tension is subject to the SAME kind of variable unwind dynamics (changing as the package empties, plus unwind speed/inertia effects), while a positive weft feeder PRE-MEASURES and stores each pick under controlled tension BEFORE the high-speed insertion moment, decoupling insertion consistency from supply package unwind variability — this is precisely why positive feeders became standard on modern high-speed shuttleless looms, directly improving pick-to-pick tension consistency and therefore PPI/fabric quality consistency.', 3, '{"positive-weft-feeder"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving mill''s fabric inspection finds a recurring defect: a very faint, only-visible-at-certain-angles horizontal line appearing at EXACTLY the same pick-count interval on every single fabric roll from ONE specific loom, regardless of which weft yarn lot/package is used. Since the defect persists across different weft lots (ruling out a weft-material cause) but recurs at a fixed INTERVAL (not a fixed elapsed-time), what should be investigated?', '["The dye recipe used at the dye house, since the defect''s persistence across weft lots proves it must originate downstream of weaving entirely","A specific MECHANICAL component on that loom with a rotational or cyclical action matching that exact pick-count period (e.g., a slightly eccentric or worn gear/cam in the beating, let-off, or take-up mechanism that completes one full cycle every N picks) — since the defect recurs at a fixed PICK-COUNT interval (not fixed elapsed time or fixed length), and is independent of weft material, a cyclically-repeating MECHANICAL cause on that specific loom is the most probable root cause","This pattern has no logical root cause and must be dismissed as an unexplainable random occurrence with zero investigable pattern","The defect must originate from the fabric''s finishing process, entirely unrelated to anything happening during the actual weaving stage"]'::jsonb, 'A specific MECHANICAL component on that loom with a rotational or cyclical action matching that exact pick-count period (e.g., a slightly eccentric or worn gear/cam in the beating, let-off, or take-up mechanism that completes one full cycle every N picks) — since the defect recurs at a fixed PICK-COUNT interval (not fixed elapsed time or fixed length), and is independent of weft material, a cyclically-repeating MECHANICAL cause on that specific loom is the most probable root cause', 'This is an advanced pattern-based root-cause diagnostic exercise — ruling OUT weft material (defect persists across different lots) and noting the defect recurs at a fixed PICK-COUNT (not time or length) interval strongly points to a CYCLICALLY-REPEATING mechanical component on that SPECIFIC loom whose rotation period matches that exact pick interval (a worn/eccentric cam, gear, or bearing in the beating, let-off, or take-up mechanism that introduces a tiny, repeating irregularity once per full mechanical cycle) — this systematic elimination of ruled-out causes (material) combined with recognizing the SPECIFIC type of pattern (pick-count-based, not time-based) to identify the likely cause category is a sophisticated diagnostic skill beyond simply noting ''something recurs.''', 3, '{"cyclical-mechanical-defect-diagnosis"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A weaving technologist compares two methods for achieving a high-density, downproof fabric: Method 1 uses very fine, high-count yarn at moderate EPI/PPI; Method 2 uses coarser yarn at extremely high EPI/PPI, both targeting the identical final cover factor. Method 2 shows measurably LOWER loom efficiency (more frequent stoppages) than Method 1. What is the most likely mechanical explanation?', '["Loom efficiency has no relationship to EPI/PPI density or yarn count, making this observed difference purely coincidental with no mechanical explanation","Extremely high EPI/PPI (Method 2) packs warp ends and picks much more tightly together, increasing warp-to-warp and warp-to-reed friction (more ends crowded through the same reed width) and requiring more beating force per pick to achieve the target density — both factors increase mechanical stress and end-breakage risk, directly reducing loom efficiency compared to Method 1''s more moderate density achieved through finer yarn rather than extreme thread-count crowding","Coarser yarn always runs MORE efficiently than finer yarn on a loom regardless of thread density, making Method 2''s lower efficiency result technically implausible","Method 1 and Method 2 must be running on completely different loom types, making this efficiency comparison invalid regardless of the yarn/density parameters used"]'::jsonb, 'Extremely high EPI/PPI (Method 2) packs warp ends and picks much more tightly together, increasing warp-to-warp and warp-to-reed friction (more ends crowded through the same reed width) and requiring more beating force per pick to achieve the target density — both factors increase mechanical stress and end-breakage risk, directly reducing loom efficiency compared to Method 1''s more moderate density achieved through finer yarn rather than extreme thread-count crowding', 'This connects the earlier cover-factor-engineering discussion (fine yarn at high density vs. coarse yarn at extreme density, both reaching the same cover factor) to a practical WEAVING-EFFICIENCY consequence — achieving target cover through EXTREME thread-count crowding (Method 2) packs more ends through the same reed width and requires harder beating to compact each pick, both increasing mechanical stress/friction and end-breakage risk, while achieving the SAME cover factor through finer yarn at more moderate density (Method 1) avoids this extreme crowding — illustrating that the choice of HOW to reach a target cover factor (yarn fineness vs. thread density) has real, different consequences for loom efficiency, not just for the finished fabric''s weight/hand-feel as previously discussed.', 3, '{"density-method-loom-efficiency"}'::text[]),
('4b495e60-fc2b-4165-a0c7-e0b144e931f7', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A loom''s ''shed geometry'' includes ''shed angle'' (the angle formed by the two layers of warp in the open shed). A NARROWER shed angle (a ''flatter,'' less-open shed) reduces warp strain per cycle but leaves LESS clearance for the weft-inserting element to pass through. What tradeoff does a weaving technologist navigate when adjusting shed angle/height?', '["Shed angle has no relationship to either warp strain or weft insertion clearance, making this described tradeoff technically nonexistent","A wider/taller shed gives more generous clearance for reliable weft insertion (reducing insertion-related picking faults) but increases the strain/extension cycled onto every warp end each pick (potentially increasing warp fatigue/breakage over time), while a narrower/flatter shed reduces this per-cycle warp strain but tightens insertion clearance (increasing the risk of the weft-carrying element contacting/damaging warp ends during insertion) — finding the optimal shed geometry balances these two competing risk factors rather than simply maximizing or minimizing shed height","Maximizing shed height/angle to the largest mechanically possible value is always the unconditionally correct setting with zero downside for any loom or fabric type","Shed angle can only be adjusted on shuttle looms, making this tradeoff discussion technically inapplicable to modern shuttleless weaving machines"]'::jsonb, 'A wider/taller shed gives more generous clearance for reliable weft insertion (reducing insertion-related picking faults) but increases the strain/extension cycled onto every warp end each pick (potentially increasing warp fatigue/breakage over time), while a narrower/flatter shed reduces this per-cycle warp strain but tightens insertion clearance (increasing the risk of the weft-carrying element contacting/damaging warp ends during insertion) — finding the optimal shed geometry balances these two competing risk factors rather than simply maximizing or minimizing shed height', 'This extends the earlier shed-timing discussion with a related shed-GEOMETRY tradeoff — a taller/wider shed opening gives the weft-inserting element (rapier tip, air jet, projectile) more generous physical clearance, reducing the risk of it striking/damaging warp ends during insertion (connecting to the earlier shed-timing discussion''s insertion-damage concern), but this greater shed height also means each warp end is stretched/flexed MORE per pick cycle, potentially accelerating warp fatigue and breakage over the course of a long production run — correct shed angle/height setting requires balancing these two genuinely competing risk factors (insertion clearance vs. warp fatigue) rather than assuming either ''more open'' or ''more closed'' is universally better.', 3, '{"shed-angle-tradeoff"}'::text[]);
